INSERT INTO CLINICAL_COPILOT.CORE.CLINICAL_NOTE VALUES
('N001','M001','E001','2025-01-10','progress','Dr. Sarah Chen','SUBJECTIVE: 86 y/o female presents for routine hypertension follow-up. Reports occasional dizziness upon standing. Denies chest pain, SOB, or palpitations. Currently taking Lisinopril 20mg, Amlodipine 10mg, Metformin 1000mg BID, Furosemide 40mg, Warfarin 5mg, and Omeprazole 20mg daily. Reports compliance with all medications. OBJECTIVE: BP 138/82 sitting, 120/70 standing (orthostatic drop noted). HR 72 regular. Weight stable at 142 lbs. ASSESSMENT: 1. Hypertension - borderline controlled with orthostatic component, likely medication-related. 2. Polypharmacy concern - 6 active medications, consider deprescribing review. 3. CKD stage 3 - stable per last labs. PLAN: Continue current regimen. Order comprehensive metabolic panel. Discuss fall risk precautions. Return in 3 months.'),
('N002','M001','E002','2025-03-15','ER','Dr. Alan Wright','CHIEF COMPLAINT: Fall at home. HISTORY: Patient fell while getting up from chair. No LOC. Landed on right hip. Pain 6/10. No head strike per patient. EXAM: Alert, oriented x3. Right hip TTP, no deformity. ROM limited by pain. Neuro intact. IMAGING: X-ray pelvis â€” no acute fracture. ASSESSMENT: Mechanical fall, likely orthostatic hypotension secondary to polypharmacy (6+ medications including Furosemide and Lisinopril). PLAN: Discharge home with fall precautions. Recommend PCP follow-up for medication review â€” concern for overtreatment contributing to falls. PT evaluation ordered.'),
('N005','M002','E005','2025-02-28','discharge','Dr. James Wilson','DISCHARGE SUMMARY: 87 y/o male admitted for acute on chronic systolic heart failure exacerbation. BNP 890 on admission. Presented with 3-day history of worsening dyspnea, 8-lb weight gain, and bilateral lower extremity edema. Treated with IV furosemide with good diuresis (net -4L over 3 days). Digoxin level checked and was therapeutic at 1.8 ng/mL. Currently on 8 medications including Warfarin for A-fib â€” INR therapeutic at 2.8. Diabetes management suboptimal with HbA1c 8.1% â€” Glipizide increased to 10mg daily. DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS: Metoprolol 50mg BID, Lisinopril 10mg daily, Warfarin 7.5mg daily, Furosemide 80mg BID, Metformin 500mg BID, Glipizide 10mg daily, Atorvastatin 80mg daily, Aspirin 81mg daily, Digoxin 0.125mg daily. Daily weights and 1500mL fluid restriction. Follow-up in 1 week.'),
('N016','M006','E016','2025-01-08','progress','Dr. James Wilson','SUBJECTIVE: 91 y/o male with Alzheimer disease, here for quarterly dementia evaluation. Caregiver (daughter) reports increased confusion over past month, occasional agitation in evenings (sundowning). Has had 2 near-falls in the past 2 weeks. Currently taking 9 medications. Patient is mostly non-verbal today. OBJECTIVE: MMSE 12/30 (prev 15/30 six months ago). Gait unsteady, uses walker. ASSESSMENT: 1. Alzheimer disease â€” progressive decline. MMSE dropped 3 points in 6 months. 2. CRITICAL POLYPHARMACY â€” 9 active medications in 91-year-old with dementia. Warfarin + Aspirin combination raises significant bleeding risk. Sertraline may worsen confusion/falls. 3. Fall risk â€” HIGH. PLAN: Urgent deprescribing review. Consider stopping Aspirin (dual antithrombotic unnecessary with Warfarin alone). Evaluate if Sertraline benefits outweigh risks. Refer to falls clinic. Caregiver education on safety measures.'),
('N025','M010','E025','2025-01-25','progress','Dr. James Wilson','SUBJECTIVE: 87 y/o male with complex cardiac history presents for heart failure management. Currently taking 10+ medications. Patient reports fatigue and mild ankle swelling. Continues on Digoxin 0.125mg daily â€” states he has been taking it without interruption. Denies missed doses of any medications. OBJECTIVE: BP 110/68, HR 62 regular. JVD +2cm. Bilateral ankle edema 1+. BNP 1250 (elevated). ASSESSMENT: 1. Heart failure â€” NYHA class III, suboptimally controlled. BNP significantly elevated. 2. CKD stage 4 â€” eGFR 22, creatinine 2.8. Requires dose adjustment consideration. 3. Polypharmacy â€” 10 active medications, HIGH risk. 4. Note: Patient reports continuing Digoxin which warrants verification with pharmacy records. Digoxin level should be monitored closely given renal function. PLAN: Increase Furosemide. Check Digoxin level. Renal function monitoring. Follow-up 2 weeks.'),
('N036','M014','E036','2025-01-05','progress','Dr. James Wilson','SUBJECTIVE: 89 y/o male with advanced Alzheimer disease. Caregiver reports worsening behavioral disturbance â€” hitting, yelling at night. NKDA. Currently on 8 medications including Lorazepam 0.5mg BID for agitation. OBJECTIVE: Agitated during exam. MMSE not testable. Gait requires 2-person assist. ASSESSMENT: 1. Alzheimer disease with behavioral disturbance â€” worsening despite Lorazepam. 2. CRITICAL CONCERN: Lorazepam in elderly dementia patient â€” Beers Criteria inappropriate, increases fall risk and paradoxical agitation. 3. Polypharmacy â€” 8 active medications. Aspirin + Lorazepam combination is concerning in fall-risk patient. PLAN: Taper Lorazepam gradually over 4 weeks. Consider low-dose quetiapine for behavioral management. Urgent fall risk assessment. Goals of care discussion with family.'),
('N004','M002','E004','2025-01-20','progress','Dr. James Wilson','SUBJECTIVE: 87 y/o male presents for coronary artery disease follow-up. Reports stable exertional symptoms. Walking 1 block before mild dyspnea. No chest pain. Compliance with 8 medications reported. OBJECTIVE: BP 128/76, HR 64 regular. No JVD. Lungs clear. ASSESSMENT: 1. CAD â€” stable on current medical therapy. 2. HFrEF â€” NYHA class II-III. 3. Type 2 DM â€” HbA1c 8.1%, above target. 4. A-fib â€” rate controlled on Metoprolol + Digoxin. INR therapeutic. PLAN: Increase Glipizide to 10mg. Continue current cardiac regimen. A1C recheck in 3 months.'),
('N010','M004','E010','2025-01-15','progress','Dr. Michael Ross','SUBJECTIVE: 84 y/o male with COPD presents for follow-up after recent exacerbation. Reports improved breathing since completing prednisone burst. Using rescue inhaler 2-3x/day (down from 6x/day). Continues Tiotropium daily. OBJECTIVE: Lungs with mild expiratory wheezing bilaterally. O2 sat 92% on room air. FEV1 1.2L (38% predicted). ASSESSMENT: 1. COPD â€” severe, improving from exacerbation but baseline is poor. 2. CAD â€” stable on Metoprolol, Lisinopril, Atorvastatin. 3. Polypharmacy â€” 7 medications. PLAN: Continue maintenance prednisone 10mg. Pulmonary rehab referral. Continue cardiac medications.'),
('N043','M016','E043','2025-05-05','discharge','Dr. Michael Ross','DISCHARGE SUMMARY: 83 y/o male admitted with acute STEMI involving LAD. Emergent PCI with DES placement to proximal LAD â€” successful with TIMI 3 flow. Peak Troponin I 15.2 ng/mL. Echo shows EF 40% with anterior wall hypokinesis. Started on dual antiplatelet therapy (Aspirin 81mg + Clopidogrel 75mg). Atorvastatin increased to 80mg. DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS: Metoprolol 50mg BID, Clopidogrel 75mg daily, Atorvastatin 80mg daily, Metformin 1000mg BID, Lisinopril 20mg daily, Aspirin 81mg daily. Cardiac rehab referral. Follow-up in 1 week.'),
('N049','M020','E049','2025-01-12','progress','Dr. Michael Ross','SUBJECTIVE: 89 y/o male with HFrEF (EF 25%), CAD, DM, CKD stage 4. Reports increasing fatigue and SOB with minimal exertion. Weight up 5 lbs in 2 weeks despite fluid restriction. Currently on 9 medications. OBJECTIVE: BP 98/60, HR 78 irregular. JVD +4cm. Bilateral LE edema 3+. Lungs with bibasilar crackles. ASSESSMENT: 1. Decompensated heart failure â€” volume overloaded despite high-dose diuretics. 2. CKD stage 4 â€” eGFR 18, limiting diuretic efficacy (cardiorenal syndrome). 3. Polypharmacy â€” 9 medications, multiple drug interactions concern (Warfarin + Aspirin bleeding risk, K+ from Spironolactone + ACE-I + CKD). 4. Hyperkalemia risk â€” K+ 5.8 on last check. PLAN: Admit for IV diuresis. Hold Spironolactone given hyperkalemia risk. Nephrology consult. Discuss goals of care â€” advanced illness trajectory.'),
('N065','M027','E065','2025-05-01','progress','Dr. Emily Park','SUBJECTIVE: 71 y/o female presents for annual wellness visit. No complaints. No chronic conditions. No medications. Active, walks 3 miles daily. OBJECTIVE: BP 118/72, HR 68. BMI 23. All systems normal. ASSESSMENT: Healthy female, no chronic diseases. PLAN: Age-appropriate screenings. Mammogram, colonoscopy due. Flu and pneumonia vaccines. Return in 1 year.'),
('N083','M038','E083','2025-06-01','progress','Dr. James Wilson','SUBJECTIVE: 70 y/o male presents for annual physical. No medications. No chronic conditions. Reports occasional lower back pain with prolonged sitting. OBJECTIVE: BP 122/78, HR 72. Normal exam. ASSESSMENT: Healthy male. Mechanical low back pain. PLAN: Core strengthening exercises. Age-appropriate cancer screenings. Return PRN or 1 year.')
